Network intrusion detection datasets are highly imbalanced, with benign traffic vastly outnumbering attack traffic. In real-world security deployments, analysts can inspect only a limited number of alerts per day making Top-K alert ranking more critical than aggregate performance metrics alone. We propose an anomaly scoring approach inspired by Natural Neighbor (NaN) relationships. In this approach, each test flow is ranked using a mutual neighbor cohesion score computed with respect to a benign reference set. The method captures the intuition that benign flows form dense and mutually consistent neighborhoods. We evaluate the approach on the CIC-IDS2017 dataset using a deployment-inspired protocol: benign traffic from Monday is used as the reference set, and traffic from DDoS, PortScan, WebAttacks, and Infiltration days is ranked by suspiciousness. We compare NaN cohesion against standard unsupervised baselines, including Local Outlier Factor (LOF), Isolation Forest (IF), kNN distance, and One-Class SVM. Results show that NaN cohesion achieves strong triage performance on DDoS attacks (PR-AUC 0.887, Precision@0.1% 0.92), while LOF performs best for WebAttacks (PR-AUC 0.997, Precision@1% 0.902). These findings suggest that neighborhood cohesion is a useful operational signal for certain attack regimes and motivate hybrid scoring approaches for structured, scan-like attacks.